  "summary": "Korean ceramic tradition characterized by white slip-painted decoration on gray stoneware.",
  "description": "Buncheong Ware features bold, dynamic designs painted with white slip onto clay bodies before glazing. This tradition emerged during the early Joseon Dynasty as a more accessible alternative to celadon, with distinctive decorative techniques including brushwork, inlay, and stamping that reflect Korean aesthetic preferences for natural forms and spontaneity.",
  "culturalContext": "Buncheong is a contemporary term for a specific type of traditional Korean stoneware that developed in the 15th century, during the Joseon period, as Goryeo celadon techniques and subject matter evolved. Buncheong ware developed from the earlier Goryeo celadon inlay technique called sanggam and rapidly distinguished itself. In contrast to the refined elegance of Goryeo celadon, buncheong is designed to be natural, unassuming, and practical. This style is characterized by its pale blue-green color and decorative techniques, which involved shaping the clay and then covering it in white slip (ceramics)  and decorating it by carving, stamping, and brushwork.",
